var e=JSON.parse(`[{"id":"06-a1b2c3d4","version":1,"setId":"set-06","domain":"Resilient Architectures","topic":"VPC design","difficulty":"easy","question":"A company creates a VPC with CIDR 10.0.0.0/16. They want to create a public subnet in us-east-1a with 256 IP addresses. Which CIDR block should be assigned to the subnet?","options":{"A":"10.0.0.0/24","B":"10.0.0.0/16","C":"10.0.0.0/28","D":"10.0.0.0/8"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A /24 subnet provides 256 IP addresses (2^8 = 256, minus 5 reserved by AWS = 251 usable). The /24 falls within the VPC's /16 CIDR (10.0.0.0/16), making it a valid subnet. AWS reserves 5 IP addresses in every subnet: the network address (.0), VPC router (.1), DNS (.2), reserved (.3), and broadcast (.255).","distractors":{"B":"10.0.0.0/16 is the VPC CIDR itself — a subnet cannot have the same CIDR as its parent VPC.","C":"A /28 provides only 16 IP addresses (11 usable after AWS reservations) — insufficient for 256 addresses.","D":"10.0.0.0/8 is larger than the VPC's /16 CIDR — subnets must be within the VPC CIDR range and cannot be larger."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/configure-subnets.html","tags":["vpc","subnets","cidr","networking"],"verified":true},{"id":"06-a1b2c3d5","version":1,"setId":"set-06","domain":"Resilient Architectures","topic":"VPC design","difficulty":"medium","question":"A company has a public subnet with an Internet Gateway attached to the VPC. EC2 instances in the public subnet cannot reach the internet. What is the most likely missing configuration?","options":{"A":"The instances lack Elastic IP addresses","B":"The public subnet route table does not have a route for 0.0.0.0/0 pointing to the Internet Gateway","C":"The security group is blocking outbound traffic","D":"The Internet Gateway is in a different AZ than the subnet"},"correct":"B","explanation":"For EC2 instances in a public subnet to reach the internet, three things must be in place: (1) an Internet Gateway attached to the VPC, (2) the subnet's route table must have a route for 0.0.0.0/0 (or ::/0 for IPv6) pointing to the Internet Gateway, and (3) the instance must have a public IP or Elastic IP. Without the route table entry, the IGW exists but traffic has no path to it.","distractors":{"A":"Elastic IPs are one way to have a persistent public IP, but EC2 instances can also use automatically assigned public IPs. The missing route is more fundamental.","C":"Security groups allow all outbound traffic by default — an outbound block is possible but less likely than a missing route table entry, which is the most common configuration oversight.","D":"Internet Gateways are VPC-level resources, not AZ-specific — they serve all subnets in the VPC regardless of AZ."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/VPC_Internet_Gateway.html","tags":["vpc","internet-gateway","route-table","public-subnet"],"verified":true},{"id":"06-a1b2c3d6","version":1,"setId":"set-06","domain":"Resilient Architectures","topic":"VPC design","difficulty":"medium","question":"A company's VPC has the CIDR 192.168.0.0/16. They need to add a secondary CIDR to allow expansion for a new microservices project. Which action enables this?","options":{"A":"Create a new VPC with the additional CIDR and peer it","B":"Associate a secondary IPv4 CIDR block with the existing VPC","C":"Change the existing VPC CIDR — CIDRs can be modified in-place","D":"Expand the existing /16 to a /8 to accommodate more addresses"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS VPCs support associating up to 5 IPv4 CIDR blocks (the original plus 4 secondary). You can associate a secondary CIDR (e.g., 10.0.0.0/16) with an existing VPC to expand its address space for new subnets. The secondary CIDR must not overlap with the primary or any peered VPC CIDRs. This is the correct way to expand VPC address space without creating a new VPC.","distractors":{"A":"Creating a new VPC and peering it works but introduces complexity (peering setup, route tables, security groups) — simply adding a secondary CIDR to the existing VPC is far simpler.","C":"VPC CIDRs cannot be modified once created — you cannot change the primary CIDR. You can only add secondary CIDRs or delete secondary CIDRs.","D":"You cannot expand a CIDR in-place — a /16 cannot be changed to /8. Only secondary CIDRs can be added."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/configure-your-vpc.html#add-cidr-block-restrictions","tags":["vpc","cidr","secondary-cidr","expansion"],"verified":true},{"id":"06-a1b2c3d7","version":1,"setId":"set-06","domain":"Resilient Architectures","topic":"VPC design","difficulty":"hard","question":"A company has a VPC with public and private subnets across 3 AZs. Private subnets route to the internet through 3 NAT Gateways (one per AZ). A security audit identifies that all traffic from private instances passes through NAT Gateway before reaching S3. How can this traffic be routed directly to S3 without going through NAT?","options":{"A":"Move S3 buckets into the VPC using S3 on Outposts","B":"Create an S3 Gateway VPC Endpoint and add a route to private subnet route tables","C":"Enable S3 Transfer Acceleration which bypasses NAT automatically","D":"Use VPC Peering to connect the VPC directly to S3"},"correct":"B","explanation":"An S3 Gateway VPC Endpoint creates a private route from the VPC to S3 without using NAT Gateway, Internet Gateway, or VPN. After creating the endpoint, add a route to the private subnet route tables pointing S3 prefix lists to the VPC endpoint. Traffic to S3 now flows directly over the AWS network — bypassing NAT Gateway, eliminating NAT Gateway data processing charges, and improving security by keeping traffic on the AWS backbone.","distractors":{"A":"S3 on Outposts stores data on Outposts hardware in your data center — it does not route traffic around NAT within AWS.","C":"S3 Transfer Acceleration optimizes cross-region or internet uploads using CloudFront edge locations — it does not bypass NAT Gateway and actually adds cost.","D":"VPC Peering connects VPCs to other VPCs — you cannot peer a VPC with S3. S3 is accessed via VPC endpoints or internet."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/privatelink/vpc-endpoints-s3.html","tags":["vpc","s3","gateway-endpoint","nat-gateway","cost"],"verified":true},{"id":"06-b1c2d3e4","version":1,"setId":"set-06","domain":"Resilient Architectures","topic":"VPC Peering vs Transit Gateway","difficulty":"easy","question":"Company A has a VPC (10.0.0.0/16) and Company B has a VPC (172.16.0.0/16). They need EC2 instances in each VPC to communicate privately using private IPs. Neither company wants to route traffic over the internet. Which AWS feature enables this?","options":{"A":"AWS Site-to-Site VPN between the two VPCs","B":"VPC Peering between the two VPCs","C":"AWS Direct Connect between the two VPCs","D":"Internet Gateway with security group restrictions"},"correct":"B","explanation":"VPC Peering creates a direct private network connection between two VPCs, allowing instances to communicate using private IP addresses. Traffic does not traverse the public internet — it stays on the AWS backbone. Peering can connect VPCs in the same account, different accounts, or even different regions (inter-region peering). The CIDRs must not overlap.","distractors":{"A":"Site-to-Site VPN creates an encrypted tunnel over the internet between a VPC and an on-premises network (or another VPC via Virtual Private Gateway) — it adds overhead and is typically used for on-premises connectivity, not VPC-to-VPC.","C":"Direct Connect connects on-premises data centers to AWS over dedicated private lines — it does not connect two VPCs to each other.","D":"Internet Gateway routes traffic to the internet — using public IP addresses and IGW for VPC-to-VPC traffic is not 'private' and exposes traffic to the internet."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/peering/what-is-vpc-peering.html","tags":["vpc-peering","private-connectivity","networking"],"verified":true},{"id":"06-b1c2d3e5","version":1,"setId":"set-06","domain":"Resilient Architectures","topic":"VPC Peering vs Transit Gateway","difficulty":"medium","question":"A company has 50 VPCs that all need to communicate with each other and with a shared services VPC (Active Directory, DNS, logging). Setting up VPC peering for all 50 VPCs would require 1,225 peering connections. Which AWS service simplifies this hub-and-spoke connectivity?","options":{"A":"VPC Peering with route table automation via Lambda","B":"AWS Transit Gateway","C":"AWS PrivateLink for each service","D":"Multiple Internet Gateways with security groups"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Transit Gateway acts as a cloud router — a central hub that routes traffic between VPCs, VPNs, and Direct Connect connections. Instead of 1,225 VPC peering connections (n*(n-1)/2 for full mesh), each VPC connects to Transit Gateway with a single attachment. The Transit Gateway route tables control routing between VPCs. This dramatically simplifies network management for large numbers of VPCs.","distractors":{"A":"Lambda automation can create peering connections programmatically but the fundamental scaling problem remains — 1,225 peering connections create 1,225 route table entries and management complexity.","C":"AWS PrivateLink exposes specific services privately without VPC peering — it does not provide general VPC-to-VPC routing for all services.","D":"Internet Gateways route traffic via the public internet — using public IPs for VPC-to-VPC communication is not private networking."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/transit-gateway/latest/tgw-ug/what-is-transit-gateway.html","tags":["transit-gateway","vpc","hub-and-spoke","scalability"],"verified":true},{"id":"06-b1c2d3e6","version":1,"setId":"set-06","domain":"Resilient Architectures","topic":"VPC Peering vs Transit Gateway","difficulty":"hard","question":"VPC A (10.0.0.0/16) is peered with VPC B (172.16.0.0/16). VPC B is peered with VPC C (192.168.0.0/16). An EC2 instance in VPC A wants to reach an EC2 instance in VPC C. Why does this fail?","options":{"A":"VPC Peering does not support inter-region connectivity","B":"VPC Peering is non-transitive — traffic from VPC A cannot traverse through VPC B to reach VPC C","C":"The CIDR blocks overlap between VPC A and VPC C","D":"VPC Peering requires both VPCs to be in the same AWS account"},"correct":"B","explanation":"VPC Peering is non-transitive — even if A-B and B-C peering connections exist, there is no automatic route for A→B→C traffic. To allow VPC A to communicate with VPC C, a direct peering connection between A and C must be established. For scenarios requiring transitive routing between many VPCs, Transit Gateway is the solution (which is explicitly transitive).","distractors":{"A":"VPC Peering does support inter-region peering — the non-transitivity is the actual limitation, not regional scope.","C":"10.0.0.0/16 and 192.168.0.0/16 do not overlap — the CIDRs are distinct. Overlapping CIDRs would prevent the peering from being established in the first place.","D":"VPC Peering supports cross-account peering — the limitation is non-transitivity, not account ownership."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/peering/peering-configurations-transitive.html","tags":["vpc-peering","transitive","routing","transit-gateway"],"verified":true},{"id":"06-c1d2e3f4","version":1,"setId":"set-06","domain":"Resilient Architectures","topic":"Direct Connect and VPN","difficulty":"easy","question":"A company needs a private, dedicated network connection from their data center to AWS with consistent low latency and high bandwidth (10 Gbps) for a financial trading application. Which connectivity option is most appropriate?","options":{"A":"AWS Site-to-Site VPN","B":"AWS Direct Connect","C":"AWS Client VPN","D":"Internet with HTTPS"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Direct Connect provides a dedicated private network connection from an on-premises location to AWS over a 1 Gbps or 10 Gbps (or 100 Gbps) physical fiber connection at a Direct Connect location. Unlike VPN (which traverses the public internet), Direct Connect offers predictable, consistent latency and does not compete with other internet traffic — essential for latency-sensitive financial applications.","distractors":{"A":"Site-to-Site VPN creates an encrypted tunnel over the public internet — bandwidth is limited to 1.25 Gbps per tunnel and latency is variable due to internet routing. Not appropriate for high-bandwidth, consistent-latency requirements.","C":"AWS Client VPN is for individual user/device access to VPC resources — not for data center to AWS connectivity at 10 Gbps.","D":"Public internet over HTTPS provides no latency guarantee, competes with other internet traffic, and cannot deliver consistent 10 Gbps throughput."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/directconnect/latest/UserGuide/Welcome.html","tags":["direct-connect","vpn","connectivity","latency","bandwidth"],"verified":true},{"id":"06-c1d2e3f5","version":1,"setId":"set-06","domain":"Resilient Architectures","topic":"Direct Connect and VPN","difficulty":"medium","question":"A company uses a single AWS Direct Connect connection for hybrid cloud connectivity. They are concerned about what happens if the Direct Connect physical connection fails. What is the AWS-recommended approach for Direct Connect resiliency?","options":{"A":"Enable Multi-AZ on the Direct Connect connection","B":"Add a Site-to-Site VPN as a backup path over the internet","C":"Use Route 53 health checks to fail over to a secondary region","D":"Direct Connect has built-in redundancy — no additional configuration needed"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS recommends configuring a Site-to-Site VPN as a backup for Direct Connect connections. If the Direct Connect link fails, BGP routing automatically switches traffic to the VPN tunnel (which traverses the internet). For maximum resilience, AWS also recommends a second Direct Connect connection at a different Direct Connect location (different physical infrastructure) in addition to the VPN backup.","distractors":{"A":"Direct Connect connections do not have a 'Multi-AZ' option — they are physical connections that exist at specific Direct Connect locations. AZ-level redundancy is for services like RDS and ALB, not Direct Connect.","C":"Route 53 health checks and failover routing address DNS-level failover to different regions — they do not provide network path redundancy for the hybrid connection itself.","D":"A single Direct Connect connection is a single point of failure — AWS explicitly recommends additional resiliency configurations."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/directconnect/latest/UserGuide/resilency_toolkit.html","tags":["direct-connect","vpn","resiliency","backup","hybrid"],"verified":true},{"id":"06-c1d2e3f6","version":1,"setId":"set-06","domain":"Resilient Architectures","topic":"Direct Connect and VPN","difficulty":"hard","question":"A company uses Direct Connect to connect their data center to their VPC in us-east-1. They want to use the same Direct Connect connection to also access their VPC in eu-west-1 without establishing a separate Direct Connect connection in Europe. Which AWS feature enables this?","options":{"A":"VPC Peering between us-east-1 and eu-west-1 VPCs, accessed via the existing Direct Connect","B":"Direct Connect Gateway associated with Virtual Private Gateways in both regions","C":"Transit Gateway in us-east-1 with VPC peering to eu-west-1","D":"AWS Global Accelerator routing Direct Connect to eu-west-1"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Direct Connect Gateway allows a single Direct Connect connection to access VPCs in multiple AWS regions globally. You associate the Direct Connect Gateway with Virtual Private Gateways (VGWs) in each VPC/region. Traffic from the on-premises data center flows over the single Direct Connect connection → Direct Connect Gateway → VGW → VPC in any associated region. This avoids the need for a separate Direct Connect connection at each region.","distractors":{"A":"VPC Peering between regions allows inter-VPC communication, but on-premises traffic can only reach the VPC directly attached to the Direct Connect connection — peering is non-transitive for Direct Connect.","C":"Transit Gateway in us-east-1 handles routing within that region — you would need a separate Direct Connect connection or Transit Gateway peering across regions, which is more complex than Direct Connect Gateway.","D":"Global Accelerator routes internet user traffic to AWS endpoints — it does not route Direct Connect on-premises traffic and is a different networking construct entirely."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/directconnect/latest/UserGuide/direct-connect-gateways-intro.html","tags":["direct-connect","direct-connect-gateway","multi-region","vgw"],"verified":true},{"id":"06-d1e2f3g4","version":1,"setId":"set-06","domain":"Resilient Architectures","topic":"Route 53 advanced routing","difficulty":"easy","question":"A company serves users from two AWS regions: us-east-1 and ap-southeast-1. Users in Asia should be routed to the Asian region and users in the US should be routed to the US region by default. Users from other continents should go to the US region. Which Route 53 routing policy implements this?","options":{"A":"Latency-based routing","B":"Geolocation routing","C":"Geoproximity routing","D":"Weighted routing"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Route 53 Geolocation routing routes traffic based on the geographic location (continent or country) of the DNS query origin. You define records for specific continents/countries and a default record for all other locations. Traffic from Asia routes to ap-southeast-1 and traffic from North America routes to us-east-1, with the US record serving as the default for all other continents. This is deterministic based on geographic origin.","distractors":{"A":"Latency-based routing measures the network round-trip time from the user to each region and routes to the lowest-latency region — this often produces the same result but is based on measured latency, not geographic mapping.","C":"Geoproximity routing routes based on geographic location AND allows biasing — it uses Traffic Flow and is used to shift traffic toward or away from specific regions by adjusting bias values.","D":"Weighted routing distributes traffic in specified percentages across records — it does not consider user location."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/routing-policy-geo.html","tags":["route53","geolocation","routing","multi-region"],"verified":true},{"id":"06-d1e2f3g5","version":1,"setId":"set-06","domain":"Resilient Architectures","topic":"Route 53 advanced routing","difficulty":"medium","question":"A company runs an active-active multi-region application with endpoints in us-east-1, eu-west-1, and ap-southeast-1. They want to route each user to the region with the lowest network latency. If a region's health check fails, users should automatically be rerouted to the next lowest-latency healthy region. Which Route 53 configuration achieves this?","options":{"A":"Geolocation routing with failover to a default record","B":"Latency-based routing with Route 53 health checks on each record","C":"Weighted routing (33/33/33) with failover routing","D":"Simple routing with multiple values (Route 53 returns all IPs)"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Route 53 latency-based routing routes each user to the region providing the lowest latency. When combined with health checks on each latency record, Route 53 automatically excludes unhealthy endpoints from responses. If us-east-1 fails its health check, Route 53 returns the next-lowest-latency healthy record (eu-west-1 or ap-southeast-1) for that user's location — providing both performance optimization and automatic failover.","distractors":{"A":"Geolocation routing determines routing based on the user's geographic location, not measured latency — users may not be routed to the closest or fastest region.","C":"Weighted routing distributes traffic by percentage — it does not optimize for user latency and requires manual weight adjustment. Failover routing (primary/secondary) is separate from weighted routing.","D":"Simple routing with multiple values returns all IPs to the client and the client selects one randomly — no health checking, no latency optimization."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/routing-policy-latency.html","tags":["route53","latency-routing","health-checks","failover","multi-region"],"verified":true},{"id":"06-d1e2f3g6","version":1,"setId":"set-06","domain":"Resilient Architectures","topic":"Route 53 advanced routing","difficulty":"hard","question":"A company wants to gradually shift 10% of traffic to a new version of their application in a blue/green deployment. Currently, all traffic goes to 'blue' (weight 100). They add a 'green' record with weight 10. What percentage of traffic goes to green?","options":{"A":"10% — the green record has weight 10 out of a total weight of 100","B":"9.09% — weight 10 / (weight 100 + weight 10) = 10/110 ≈ 9.09%","C":"50% — Route 53 splits traffic equally between two records","D":"10% — Route 53 interprets weights as absolute percentages"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Route 53 weighted routing calculates the fraction of traffic as (this record's weight) / (sum of all record weights). With blue=100 and green=10, total weight = 110. Green receives 10/110 ≈ 9.09% of traffic, and blue receives 100/110 ≈ 90.91%. Weights are relative ratios, not absolute percentages. To achieve exactly 10%, set blue=90 and green=10 (10/100 = 10%).","distractors":{"A":"10% is not correct because Route 53 divides by the sum of all weights, not by the highest weight. 10/(100+10) = 9.09%.","C":"Route 53 does not split traffic equally between records — it uses the weight ratios.","D":"Route 53 weights are relative ratios, not absolute percentages — weight 10 out of total 110 is 9.09%, not 10%."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/routing-policy-weighted.html","tags":["route53","weighted-routing","blue-green","deployment","traffic-shifting"],"verified":true},{"id":"06-e1f2g3h4","version":1,"setId":"set-06","domain":"Resilient Architectures","topic":"ALB content-based routing","difficulty":"easy","question":"A company has a microservices application where \`/api/users/*\` requests go to the User Service and \`/api/orders/*\` requests go to the Order Service. Both services run behind the same ALB. Which ALB feature routes requests to the correct service?","options":{"A":"ALB weighted target groups","B":"ALB listener rules with path-based routing conditions","C":"ALB host-based routing with different domains","D":"ALB security groups filtering by path"},"correct":"B","explanation":"ALB listener rules support path-based routing conditions that match the URL path of incoming requests. A rule with condition \`path is /api/users/*\` forwards to the User Service target group, and a rule with condition \`path is /api/orders/*\` forwards to the Order Service target group. This allows a single ALB to route to multiple microservices based on the request URL path.","distractors":{"A":"Weighted target groups distribute traffic between multiple target groups in defined proportions — they are used for canary deployments or blue/green, not for routing to different services based on path.","C":"Host-based routing routes based on the HTTP Host header (domain name), not the URL path — it would require different domain names for each service.","D":"Security groups control network access (allow/deny based on IP/port) — they cannot parse or route based on URL paths."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/elasticloadbalancing/latest/application/listener-update-rules.html","tags":["alb","path-routing","microservices","listener-rules"],"verified":true},{"id":"06-e1f2g3h5","version":1,"setId":"set-06","domain":"Resilient Architectures","topic":"ALB content-based routing","difficulty":"medium","question":"A company hosts multiple websites on EC2 instances behind a single ALB. \`app1.example.com\` should route to the App1 target group and \`app2.example.com\` should route to the App2 target group. Both use the same HTTPS listener on port 443. What ALB feature enables this, and what additional requirement must be met?","options":{"A":"Path-based routing — add rules for \`/app1/\` and \`/app2/\` paths","B":"Host-based routing — ALB matches the HTTP Host header; ACM must have certificates for both domains (or a wildcard cert)","C":"Weighted routing — assign weights 50/50 between the two target groups","D":"Cross-zone load balancing — enable in the ALB settings"},"correct":"B","explanation":"ALB host-based routing matches the HTTP \`Host\` header of incoming requests to route to different target groups. A rule \`Host is app1.example.com\` forwards to App1's target group and \`Host is app2.example.com\` forwards to App2's target group. For HTTPS, the ALB's SSL certificate must cover both domains — achieved with separate ACM certificates (via SNI, ALB supports up to 25 certificates per listener) or a wildcard certificate for *.example.com.","distractors":{"A":"Path-based routing routes based on the URL path (e.g., /api/), not the hostname — for separate domains, host-based routing is appropriate.","C":"Weighted routing distributes traffic proportionally between target groups — it does not differentiate between the two hostnames; both hostnames would receive a mix of app1 and app2 responses.","D":"Cross-zone load balancing distributes traffic evenly across instances in all AZs — it does not perform content-based routing by hostname."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/elasticloadbalancing/latest/application/load-balancer-listeners.html#host-conditions","tags":["alb","host-routing","sni","acm","https"],"verified":true},{"id":"06-e1f2g3h6","version":1,"setId":"set-06","domain":"Resilient Architectures","topic":"ALB content-based routing","difficulty":"hard","question":"An application uses an ALB. A new \`/admin\` path must only be accessible from a specific corporate IP range (203.0.113.0/24) and should return 403 for all other source IPs, without deploying WAF. Which ALB listener rule configuration achieves this?","options":{"A":"Create an ALB security group rule allowing only 203.0.113.0/24 on port 443","B":"Create two ALB listener rules: rule 1 matches path '/admin*' AND source IP '203.0.113.0/24' → forward to admin target group; rule 2 matches path '/admin*' → return 403 fixed response","C":"Create a NACL blocking all non-corporate IPs at the subnet level","D":"Use ALB sticky sessions tied to the corporate IP range"},"correct":"B","explanation":"ALB listener rules support multiple conditions combined with AND logic. Rule 1 uses both path condition (\`/admin*\`) AND source IP condition (\`203.0.113.0/24\`) with lower priority to forward to the admin target group. Rule 2 matches only the path (\`/admin*\`) with higher priority for non-matching IPs, returning a 403 fixed response. Since rules are evaluated in priority order, corporate IPs match rule 1 (forward) while all others fall through to rule 2 (403).","distractors":{"A":"ALB security groups allow all traffic through or block at port level — they cannot differentiate by URL path. This would block ALL traffic from non-corporate IPs, not just /admin paths.","C":"NACLs operate at the subnet level with source IP filtering — they cannot filter by URL path, so all corporate-IP traffic would be allowed and all others blocked from the entire application, not just /admin.","D":"ALB sticky sessions maintain session affinity to a specific target based on cookies — they do not perform IP-based access control."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/elasticloadbalancing/latest/application/listener-update-rules.html","tags":["alb","listener-rules","ip-filtering","fixed-response","security"],"verified":true},{"id":"06-f1g2h3i4","version":1,"setId":"set-06","domain":"Resilient Architectures","topic":"NLB and static IPs","difficulty":"medium","question":"A company's partner requires a firewall whitelist of static IP addresses to allow traffic from the company's load balancer. The application needs to handle millions of TCP connections per second with ultra-low latency. Which load balancer type provides static IP addresses per AZ?","options":{"A":"Application Load Balancer with Elastic IPs assigned","B":"Network Load Balancer — each NLB node in each AZ gets a static IP or assigned Elastic IP","C":"Classic Load Balancer with EIP assignment","D":"API Gateway with a static IP allocation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Network Load Balancers provide a static IP address per AZ automatically. You can also assign Elastic IP addresses to NLB nodes, giving partners a fixed, predictable IP whitelist. NLB operates at Layer 4 (TCP/UDP/TLS) and is designed for extreme performance — millions of requests per second with ultra-low latency (sub-millisecond). This makes NLB the only AWS load balancer type with static IPs.","distractors":{"A":"Application Load Balancers use dynamic IP addresses that change over time and cannot have Elastic IPs directly assigned to the ALB nodes — their IPs are not static. (You can put NLB in front of ALB to get static IPs for an ALB.)","C":"Classic Load Balancers are the legacy option and do not support static or Elastic IP assignment to load balancer nodes.","D":"API Gateway is an API management service, not a load balancer — it does not provide static IPs for TCP load balancing at the connection level."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/elasticloadbalancing/latest/network/introduction.html","tags":["nlb","static-ip","elastic-ip","layer4","whitelist"],"verified":true},{"id":"06-f1g2h3i5","version":1,"setId":"set-06","domain":"Resilient Architectures","topic":"NLB and static IPs","difficulty":"hard","question":"An application uses an NLB in front of EC2 instances. The application needs to see the actual client source IP address in the application logs, not the NLB's IP. Which NLB feature preserves the client source IP?","options":{"A":"Enable Proxy Protocol v2 on the NLB target group, then parse the PP2 header in the application","B":"Enable X-Forwarded-For header on the NLB — it works the same as ALB","C":"NLB automatically preserves the client IP at Layer 4 — no configuration needed for TCP targets","D":"Deploy WAF in front of the NLB to inject client IP headers"},"correct":"C","explanation":"For TCP and UDP targets (EC2 instances directly), NLB automatically preserves the client source IP address at Layer 4 — the instance sees the actual client IP as the source in the TCP connection, with no additional configuration. This is a fundamental difference from ALB, which terminates connections and uses X-Forwarded-For. For TLS termination at the NLB, Proxy Protocol v2 must be enabled to pass client IP information.","distractors":{"A":"Proxy Protocol v2 is needed when TLS is terminated at the NLB (not the target) — for plain TCP targets, the client IP is preserved natively without Proxy Protocol.","B":"X-Forwarded-For is an HTTP header used by ALB — NLB operates at Layer 4 (TCP/UDP) and does not add HTTP headers. NLB preserves the original TCP connection source IP directly.","D":"WAF operates at Layer 7 (HTTP) and is designed for security rule enforcement — it cannot inject TCP-level source IP information."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/elasticloadbalancing/latest/network/load-balancer-target-groups.html#client-ip-preservation","tags":["nlb","source-ip","proxy-protocol","client-ip","layer4"],"verified":true},{"id":"06-g1h2i3j4","version":1,"setId":"set-06","domain":"Resilient Architectures","topic":"Egress-only Internet Gateway","difficulty":"easy","question":"A company deploys EC2 instances with IPv6 addresses in private subnets. The instances need to initiate outbound connections to the internet (e.g., to download patches) but must not be reachable from the internet over IPv6. Which gateway enables this?","options":{"A":"NAT Gateway configured for IPv6 traffic","B":"Internet Gateway with restrictive NACLs","C":"Egress-Only Internet Gateway","D":"IPv6 VPN endpoint"},"correct":"C","explanation":"An Egress-Only Internet Gateway (EIGW) enables outbound IPv6 traffic from private subnets to the internet while preventing inbound IPv6 connections from the internet. It is the IPv6 equivalent of a NAT Gateway for IPv4 (but note: EIGW has no cost for data processing — you only pay for the EC2 instance's data transfer). EIGW is stateful — return traffic from established outbound connections is allowed automatically.","distractors":{"A":"NAT Gateway only supports IPv4 — it cannot be used for IPv6 traffic. There is no IPv6 NAT in AWS (IPv6 addresses are globally unique and do not need address translation).","B":"An Internet Gateway allows both inbound and outbound IPv6 traffic — NACLs could block inbound but this is a less elegant solution than EIGW, which natively prevents inbound.","D":"There is no 'IPv6 VPN endpoint' service in AWS for this purpose — EIGW is the purpose-built solution."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/egress-only-internet-gateway.html","tags":["eigw","ipv6","egress","private-subnet","nat"],"verified":true},{"id":"06-h1i2j3k4","version":1,"setId":"set-06","domain":"Resilient Architectures","topic":"VPC Flow Logs","difficulty":"easy","question":"A security team needs to audit all network traffic flowing through a VPC, including rejected connections, for a 90-day compliance audit. Where can VPC Flow Logs be stored for long-term retention and analysis?","options":{"A":"EC2 instance local storage","B":"Amazon S3 or Amazon CloudWatch Logs","C":"AWS CloudTrail","D":"Amazon DynamoDB"},"correct":"B","explanation":"VPC Flow Logs can be published to Amazon CloudWatch Logs for real-time analysis and alerting, or to Amazon S3 for cost-effective long-term storage. For a 90-day compliance requirement, S3 with a lifecycle policy (or CloudWatch Logs with a 90-day retention period) satisfies the requirement. Flow logs capture accepted and rejected traffic, source/destination IPs, ports, protocol, and packet/byte counts.","distractors":{"A":"Flow logs are published by the VPC Flow Logs service to CloudWatch or S3 — they cannot be directly written to EC2 instance storage.","C":"AWS CloudTrail captures API calls to AWS services (control plane), not VPC network traffic data plane flows — they serve different auditing purposes.","D":"DynamoDB is not a destination for VPC Flow Logs — the service supports CloudWatch Logs and S3 as destinations."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/flow-logs.html","tags":["vpc-flow-logs","security","audit","s3","cloudwatch"],"verified":true},{"id":"06-h1i2j3k5","version":1,"setId":"set-06","domain":"Resilient Architectures","topic":"VPC Flow Logs","difficulty":"medium","question":"A security team analyzes VPC Flow Logs and notices traffic between two EC2 instances in the same VPC security group. The flow log shows \`REJECT\` for traffic on port 3306 (MySQL). What is the most likely cause?","options":{"A":"The Internet Gateway is blocking the MySQL port","B":"The security group allows port 3306 inbound only from specific CIDR ranges, and the source instance's IP is not in the allowed range","C":"VPC Flow Logs only capture ACCEPT traffic — REJECT indicates the log is corrupted","D":"MySQL traffic cannot cross AZs within a VPC"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A REJECT in VPC Flow Logs means either the security group did not allow the traffic or a NACL denied it. Security groups are stateful and evaluated per-connection. If the MySQL security group allows port 3306 only from a specific CIDR (e.g., 10.0.1.0/24) but the source instance has an IP outside that range, the connection is rejected. Even instances in the same security group may face this if the security group's inbound rules don't permit self-referencing or the specific source IP range.","distractors":{"A":"Internet Gateways route traffic between the VPC and the internet — they do not filter by port or inspect traffic between instances within the VPC.","C":"VPC Flow Logs capture both ACCEPT and REJECT traffic — REJECT records are valid and essential for security analysis.","D":"MySQL traffic (or any TCP/UDP traffic) flows freely across AZs within a VPC — there are no AZ-based networking restrictions within a single VPC."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/flow-logs.html#flow-log-records","tags":["vpc-flow-logs","security-groups","reject","mysql","troubleshooting"],"verified":true},{"id":"06-h1i2j3k6","version":1,"setId":"set-06","domain":"Resilient Architectures","topic":"VPC Flow Logs","difficulty":"hard","question":"A company wants to detect when EC2 instances make outbound connections to known malicious IP addresses using VPC Flow Logs. Which AWS service combination can automate this detection?","options":{"A":"VPC Flow Logs → S3 → manual periodic review","B":"VPC Flow Logs → CloudWatch Logs → CloudWatch Metric Filter + Alarm for specific IPs","C":"Amazon GuardDuty — it analyzes VPC Flow Logs, DNS logs, and CloudTrail to detect threats automatically","D":"AWS Security Hub with a custom rule scanning Flow Log S3 objects"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon GuardDuty automatically ingests and analyzes VPC Flow Logs, CloudTrail, DNS query logs, and other sources using machine learning and threat intelligence (including known malicious IP lists from AWS and third parties). GuardDuty generates findings like 'UnauthorizedAccess:EC2/MaliciousIPCaller' when instances communicate with known malicious IPs — without any manual configuration of specific IP addresses. This is far more scalable and accurate than manual CloudWatch metric filters.","distractors":{"A":"Manual periodic review is reactive and cannot provide real-time automated detection — insufficient for security monitoring.","B":"CloudWatch Metric Filters require manually specifying which IP addresses to detect — maintaining a current list of millions of malicious IPs is impractical and error-prone.","D":"Security Hub aggregates findings from multiple services (including GuardDuty) but does not natively analyze raw VPC Flow Log data to detect malicious IPs — GuardDuty does this analysis."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/guardduty/latest/ug/guardduty_data-sources.html","tags":["guardduty","vpc-flow-logs","threat-detection","malicious-ip","security"],"verified":true},{"id":"06-i1j2k3l4","version":1,"setId":"set-06","domain":"Resilient Architectures","topic":"Transit Gateway","difficulty":"medium","question":"A company has multiple VPCs connected via Transit Gateway. They want VPCs in a 'production' group to communicate with each other and with the shared services VPC, but NOT with VPCs in the 'development' group. How is this isolation implemented in Transit Gateway?","options":{"A":"Use VPC Peering between production VPCs only, and not between dev VPCs","B":"Create separate Transit Gateway Route Tables for production and development, and configure which attachments can propagate routes to which route tables","C":"Apply security groups on the Transit Gateway to block dev-to-prod traffic","D":"Enable Transit Gateway Network Manager to separate production from development"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Transit Gateway Route Tables provide network segmentation. Create a 'production' route table with production VPC attachments and shared services attachments, and a 'development' route table with only development VPC and shared services attachments. Each VPC attachment is associated with one route table. By not propagating production VPC routes into the development route table (and vice versa), production and development VPCs cannot reach each other while both can reach shared services.","distractors":{"A":"VPC Peering alongside Transit Gateway adds complexity and defeats the purpose of TGW — TGW route tables are the correct isolation mechanism within TGW.","C":"Security groups are applied to EC2 instances and other resources within VPCs — they cannot be applied to Transit Gateway itself to filter inter-VPC traffic.","D":"Network Manager provides visibility into TGW topologies — it does not enforce network isolation between route tables."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/transit-gateway/latest/tgw-ug/tgw-route-tables.html","tags":["transit-gateway","route-tables","isolation","segmentation","production"],"verified":true},{"id":"06-i1j2k3l5","version":1,"setId":"set-06","domain":"Resilient Architectures","topic":"Transit Gateway","difficulty":"hard","question":"A company has Transit Gateway connecting VPCs in us-east-1. They acquire a company with VPCs in eu-west-1. Both companies use the 10.0.0.0/8 address space with overlapping CIDRs. They need secure connectivity between the acquisitions' specific application VPCs. Which solution handles overlapping CIDRs?","options":{"A":"Transit Gateway inter-region peering — TGW handles CIDR overlap automatically","B":"AWS PrivateLink to expose specific services from the acquired company's VPCs without routing","C":"VPC Peering with CIDR translation using NAT","D":"Direct Connect Gateway bridging the two Transit Gateways"},"correct":"B","explanation":"When VPC CIDRs overlap, standard routing (peering, TGW) cannot resolve ambiguity — packets intended for 10.1.1.5 in one company's VPC cannot be distinguished from the same IP in the other's. AWS PrivateLink solves this by exposing specific services (via NLB) through VPC Endpoints without routing — the consumer uses a private endpoint IP in their own CIDR space to reach the provider's service, regardless of CIDR overlap. Only specific services are exposed, not the entire VPC.","distractors":{"A":"Transit Gateway inter-region peering does not resolve CIDR overlaps — it still requires non-overlapping CIDRs in route tables to route traffic correctly.","C":"VPC Peering does not support CIDR overlap — you cannot create a peering connection between VPCs with overlapping CIDRs. NAT for CIDR translation is not a native AWS feature for VPC peering.","D":"Direct Connect Gateway connects on-premises to AWS VPCs — it does not bridge between AWS Transit Gateways or resolve CIDR conflicts between VPCs."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/privatelink/what-is-privatelink.html","tags":["privatelink","overlapping-cidr","transit-gateway","acquisition","networking"],"verified":true},{"id":"06-j1k2l3m4","version":1,"setId":"set-06","domain":"Resilient Architectures","topic":"CloudFront with custom origins","difficulty":"medium","question":"A company serves a web application from an on-premises data center. They want CloudFront to cache static assets globally while forwarding dynamic requests to their on-premises servers. The on-premises servers are accessible via a Direct Connect connection. Is CloudFront compatible with on-premises origins?","options":{"A":"No — CloudFront can only use S3 buckets and AWS ALBs as origins","B":"Yes — CloudFront supports any HTTP/HTTPS origin including on-premises servers accessible via public IP or Direct Connect","C":"Yes — but only if the on-premises servers have public IP addresses with valid HTTPS certificates","D":"No — CloudFront requires origins to be in the same AWS region"},"correct":"B","explanation":"CloudFront supports custom origins — any HTTP or HTTPS endpoint regardless of where it runs. Origins can be on-premises servers, third-party hosting providers, or any internet-accessible endpoint. For on-premises servers connected via Direct Connect, the origin traffic from CloudFront edge locations to the origin follows the standard internet path (since CloudFront POPs connect over the internet to origins). For secure on-premises connectivity, you can use a public IP on the on-premises server with HTTPS, or an AWS-facing interface endpoint.","distractors":{"A":"CloudFront supports S3, AWS load balancers, EC2, API Gateway, AND arbitrary HTTP/HTTPS custom origins — it is not limited to AWS services.","C":"CloudFront also supports HTTP (non-HTTPS) origins and origins with private IP addresses through VPC origins (newer feature) — public IP with HTTPS is not the only option.","D":"CloudFront origins have no regional restrictions — edges connect to origins worldwide."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudFront/latest/DeveloperGuide/DownloadDistS3AndCustomOrigins.html","tags":["cloudfront","custom-origin","on-premises","direct-connect","hybrid"],"verified":true},{"id":"06-j1k2l3m5","version":1,"setId":"set-06","domain":"Resilient Architectures","topic":"CloudFront with custom origins","difficulty":"hard","question":"A company uses CloudFront with an ALB origin. Users report that some requests bypass CloudFront and hit the ALB directly, circumventing WAF rules applied at CloudFront. How can the company prevent direct access to the ALB?","options":{"A":"Set the ALB security group to allow inbound traffic only from CloudFront's managed prefix list","B":"Move the WAF from CloudFront to the ALB — WAF rules are then enforced regardless of access path","C":"Use signed URLs for all requests to prevent direct ALB access","D":"Enable ALB access logs to detect and block direct access patterns"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS publishes CloudFront's IP ranges in a managed prefix list (\`com.amazonaws.global.cloudfront.origin-facing\`). By setting the ALB security group's inbound rule to allow port 443 (HTTPS) only from this managed prefix list, the ALB rejects all traffic that does not originate from CloudFront edge nodes. This ensures all traffic — including potential attackers trying to bypass CloudFront WAF — must pass through CloudFront.","distractors":{"B":"Moving WAF to the ALB enforces security rules regardless of access path, which is a valid defense-in-depth measure. However, this doesn't prevent the security violation (bypassing CloudFront) — the question asks how to PREVENT direct access, not just mitigate it.","C":"Signed URLs authenticate users for specific content access — they do not restrict the network path. An attacker who knows the ALB DNS name can still bypass CloudFront even with signed URL validation.","D":"Access log analysis is reactive monitoring — it detects bypass attempts after they occur rather than preventing them."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudFront/latest/DeveloperGuide/restrict-access-to-load-balancer.html","tags":["cloudfront","alb","security-group","waf","bypass-prevention"],"verified":true},{"id":"06-k1l2m3n4","version":1,"setId":"set-06","domain":"Resilient Architectures","topic":"Security Groups vs NACLs","difficulty":"medium","question":"A company needs to block all traffic from a specific IP address (203.0.113.100) from accessing any resources in a subnet, regardless of which security groups are applied. Which control achieves this at the subnet level?","options":{"A":"Add a deny rule in the security group for each EC2 instance","B":"Add a DENY rule in the Network ACL (NACL) for 203.0.113.100 with a lower rule number than the ALLOW rule","C":"Enable VPC Flow Logs to identify and report the IP","D":"Add the IP to the AWS WAF IP block list"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Network ACLs (NACLs) are stateless, subnet-level firewalls that evaluate rules in numeric order (lowest rule number first). Adding a DENY rule for 203.0.113.100 with a rule number lower than any ALLOW rule (e.g., rule 90: DENY ALL from 203.0.113.100) blocks ALL traffic from that IP before it reaches any resources in the subnet, regardless of instance security groups. NACLs are the correct tool for blanket IP blocking at the subnet level.","distractors":{"A":"Security groups do not support DENY rules — they are allow-only. You can only remove ALLOW rules; you cannot explicitly deny a specific IP.","C":"VPC Flow Logs capture traffic for analysis — they do not block any traffic.","D":"AWS WAF IP block lists work at the HTTP/HTTPS application layer (Layer 7) for ALB/CloudFront/API Gateway — they do not block TCP/UDP traffic at the subnet level for all protocols and ports."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/vpc-network-acls.html","tags":["nacl","security-groups","ip-blocking","subnet","stateless"],"verified":true},{"id":"06-k1l2m3n5","version":1,"setId":"set-06","domain":"Resilient Architectures","topic":"Security Groups vs NACLs","difficulty":"hard","question":"An EC2 instance in a private subnet has a security group allowing inbound port 443 from 0.0.0.0/0. The subnet's NACL has these rules: Rule 100: ALLOW TCP 443 inbound from 0.0.0.0/0; Rule * DENY ALL inbound. The NACL outbound rules: Rule 100: ALLOW TCP 1024-65535 outbound to 0.0.0.0/0; Rule * DENY ALL outbound. A client connects on port 443. Does the connection succeed?","options":{"A":"No — the security group blocks outbound traffic by default","B":"Yes — both the NACL and security group allow the traffic, and the NACL ephemeral port outbound rule allows return traffic","C":"No — the NACL outbound rule for port 1024-65535 is a different port than 443 and will block the return traffic","D":"Yes — NACLs are stateful, so return traffic is automatically allowed"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The connection succeeds. Inbound: NACL rule 100 allows TCP 443 inbound → security group allows TCP 443 inbound → connection reaches the instance. Return traffic: the instance responds on an ephemeral port (the client's source port, 1024-65535). The NACL outbound rule 100 allows TCP 1024-65535 outbound, permitting the response. Security groups are stateful — they automatically allow return traffic. NACLs are stateless — the outbound ephemeral port rule explicitly covers this.","distractors":{"A":"Security groups allow all outbound traffic by default — there is no default outbound deny for security groups (unlike security groups in some other cloud providers).","C":"The return traffic does use TCP 1024-65535 (ephemeral ports on the client side) — the NACL rule 100 outbound allows exactly this range. Port 443 is only the destination port for the initial inbound connection.","D":"NACLs are stateless — return traffic is NOT automatically allowed. This is why the outbound NACL rule explicitly permitting 1024-65535 is necessary."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/vpc-network-acls.html","tags":["nacl","security-groups","stateless","stateful","ephemeral-ports"],"verified":true},{"id":"06-l1m2n3o4","version":1,"setId":"set-06","domain":"Resilient Architectures","topic":"AWS PrivateLink","difficulty":"medium","question":"Company A wants to offer a SaaS service to Company B without exposing Company A's VPC CIDR or allowing general VPC peering. Company B should access only the specific service endpoint. Which AWS feature enables this?","options":{"A":"VPC Peering with restrictive route tables","B":"AWS PrivateLink — Company A creates a VPC Endpoint Service backed by an NLB; Company B creates a VPC Endpoint to connect","C":"AWS Transit Gateway with strict route table policies","D":"Direct Connect between Company A and Company B's data centers"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS PrivateLink allows service providers to expose a specific service (via a Network Load Balancer) to consumers privately, without VPC peering, internet exposure, or routing cross-VPC CIDRs. Company A creates a VPC Endpoint Service backed by an NLB. Company B creates an Interface VPC Endpoint to access the service using a private IP in their own VPC. CIDRs don't need to be known or non-overlapping — only the specific service is accessible, not the entire VPC.","distractors":{"A":"VPC Peering connects entire VPCs — even with restrictive route tables, it requires non-overlapping CIDRs and grants broader access than a single service endpoint.","C":"Transit Gateway connects entire VPCs and handles inter-VPC routing — it is not designed for selective service exposure to a consumer.","D":"Direct Connect connects on-premises data centers to AWS — it does not connect two companies' VPCs to each other without additional services."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/privatelink/privatelink-share-your-services.html","tags":["privatelink","endpoint-service","saas","cross-account","nlb"],"verified":true}]`);export{e as default};